  "evidence": "TEXT_D: Leave the EU via Article 50 and oppose an EU army; leave the ECHR and enact a UK Bill of Rights; five-year moratorium on unskilled immigration with an Australian-style points system, five years’ contributions before full benefits/NHS, revoke citizenship for terrorists, and boost Border Force; promote British values and integration with English for official documents; defence to 2%+ GDP and retain Trident; repeal the Climate Change Act and abolish the EU ETS; restrict general election voting to citizens; zero-tolerance on antisocial behaviour and more frontline police/prison/Border posts. TEXT_F: Protect the Human Rights Act; reform the EU from within; lead the low‑carbon transition with a legal 2030 decarbonised power target; keep 0.7% aid; votes at 16 and constitutional reform; community‑led, proportionate counter‑extremism; controlled but ‘fair’ immigration with exit checks, extra border staff, a two‑year wait for benefits, and English requirements.",
  "reasoning": "The electorate profile is more TAN than GAL, prioritising national sovereignty, border control, law and order, and traditional/identity commitments. TEXT_D argues directly from these TAN commitments (sovereignty over supranational bodies, restrictive immigration framed as control and identity, strong defence and policing), whereas TEXT_F foregrounds GAL commitments (rights protections, climate leadership, EU engagement) despite some administrative controls on immigration.",
  "confidence": "high",
  "winner": "TEXT_D"
}
